  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Direct and Indirect Object Pronouns",
      "content": "Object pronouns replace nouns to avoid repetition. There are two types.\n\nDirect Object Pronouns (replaces the DIRECT object — the person/thing directly affected by the verb):\n- me (me)\n- te (you, informal)\n- lo/la (him/her/it — masculine/feminine)\n- nos (us)\n- los/las (them — masculine/feminine)\n\nIndirect Object Pronouns (replaces the INDIRECT object — the person FOR WHOM the action is done):\n- me (to/for me)\n- te (to/for you, informal)\n- le (to/for him/her/you formal)\n- nos (to/for us)\n- les (to/for them/you formal plural)\n- os (to/for you plural, Spain informal)\n\nPronoun placement: BEFORE the conjugated verb, or attached to the END of an infinitive/gerund.\n\nExample: 'Veo la película' → 'La veo.' (I see the movie → I see it.)\n'Le doy el libro a María' → 'Le doy el libro.' (I give the book to María → I give her the book.)",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Pronoun",
          "Type",
          "English",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "me",
            "Direct & Indirect",
            "me / to me",
            "Me veo. / Me da el libro."
          ],
          [
            "te",
            "Direct & Indirect",
            "you / to you",
            "Te veo. / Te da el libro."
          ],
          [
            "lo/la",
            "Direct",
            "him/her/it",
            "Lo veo. / La veo."
          ],
          [
            "nos",
            "Direct & Indirect",
            "us / to us",
            "Nos vemos. / Nos da el libro."
          ],
          [
            "los/las",
            "Direct",
            "them (m/f)",
            "Los veo. / Las veo."
          ],
          [
            "le/les",
            "Indirect",
            "to him/her/them",
            "Le doy el libro."
          ],
          [
            "os",
            "Direct & Indirect",
            "you all (Spain)",
            "Os veo. / Os da el libro."
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        "¿Ves a tu hermano? — Sí, lo veo. (Do you see your brother? — Yes, I see him.)",
        "María me llama cada día. (María calls me every day.)",
        "Le di un regalo a mi madre. (I gave a gift to my mother.)",
        "¿Puedes ayudarme? (Can you help me?)"
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian has a similar system: 'mă/te/îl/îi/ne/vă/îi' for object pronouns. The Spanish lo/la distinction (masculine/feminine) is similar to Romanian îl/îi. Romanian speakers should find object pronouns much easier than English speakers."
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [
    {
      "title": "Object Pronouns — Position and Stress",
      "content": "Spanish object pronouns are unstressed syllables that attach to verbs:\n\nStandalone pronouns (always unstressed):\n- **me** = meh (me)\n- **te** = teh (you informal)\n- **lo/la** = loh/lah (him/her/it)\n- **nos** = nohs (us)\n- **los/las** = lohss/lahs (them)\n- **le/les** = leh/lehs (him/her formal)\n- **os** = ohs (you all — Spain)\n\nAttached to verbs (enclitic = after, proclitic = before):\n- **dámelo** = DAH-meh-loh (give it to me — accent when attached!)\n- **llámalo** = YAH-mah-loh (call him)\n- **envíamelo** = en-BEE-ah-meh-loh (send it to me)\n\nWhen an extra syllable is added (dár + lo = darlo), an accent mark is added to maintain original stress: dármelo, llamalo → llámalo.\n\nKey: The 'll' in 'llamar' = 'y' sound: YAH-mah.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "dámelo",
          "source": "DAH-meh-loh (give it to me — accent on 'da')"
        },
        {
          "target": "llámalo",
          "source": "YAH-mah-loh (call him — 'll' = 'y')"
        },
        {
          "target": "veo",
          "source": "BEH-oh (I see — 'v' = soft 'b', not English 'v')"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "What does the direct object pronoun 'lo' replace?",
      "options": [
        "A feminine singular noun",
        "A masculine singular noun",
        "An indirect object",
        "A plural noun"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "'Lo' replaces a masculine singular direct object: 'Veo el libro → Lo veo.'"
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: '¿Ves a Juan? — Sí, ____ veo cada día.' (Do you see Juan? — Yes, I see him every day.)",
      "correct": "lo",
      "explanation": "'Lo' replaces 'Juan' (masculine singular direct object)."
    },
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "Which pronoun is an INDIRECT object pronoun?",
      "options": [
        "lo",
        "la",
        "le",
        "los"
      ],
      "correct": 2,
      "explanation": "'Le' is an indirect object pronoun (to him/her/you formal). Lo/la/los are direct."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'María ____ llama cada día.' (María calls me every day.)",
      "correct": "me",
      "explanation": "'Me' is both a direct and indirect object pronoun. 'María me llama' = María calls me."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: '¿Puedes ____ (help) ____ (me)?' (Can you help me?)",
      "correct": "ayudarme",
      "explanation": "'Ayudarme' — the reflexive pronoun attaches to the infinitive when combined with poder: '¿Puedes ayudarme?'"
    },
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "Where does the object pronoun go with a conjugated verb?",
      "options": [
        "After the verb",
        "Before the conjugated verb",
        "At the end of the sentence",
        "It's optional"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Object pronouns go BEFORE the conjugated verb: 'Lo veo', 'La llamo', 'Me da el libro.'"
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Le di un regalo a mi ____ (mother).' (I gave a gift to my mother.)",
      "correct": "madre",
      "explanation": "'Le di un regalo a mi madre' — 'le' is the indirect object pronoun (to her). You can also add 'a mi madre' for clarity."
    }
  ]
